# Nemul - the Neki32 simulator

Two front ends over one emulation core.

    gmake            # bin/nemul.elf            graphical, SDL2 + Dear ImGui
    gmake headless   # bin/nemul-headless.elf   console, no display at all

The core - `interp.cpp`, `process.cpp`, `sysc.cpp`, `rsp.cpp`, `trace.cpp`, `prof.cpp` and the
embedded init image `init.inc` - knows nothing about either front end. What differs is the
main loop, the input, the preferences and the picture.

## Dependencies

`bin/nemul.elf` needs SDL2 (2.0.17 or later; `pkg-config sdl2` or `sdl2-config` must find it,
otherwise the Makefile assumes `/usr/local`). Dear ImGui is vendored in `third_party/imgui`.
There is no OpenGL, no native widget toolkit and no build step for either: `gmake` compiles
SDL2's two backend files and five ImGui files along with the emulator.

`bin/nemul-headless.elf` needs nothing beyond the C++ runtime and libm.

## Running

    ./bin/nemul.elf [<card image>]

A plain file name is the card image to run; `--image <iso>` does the same thing. File > Open
Image (or dropping a `.iso` on the window) inserts a card image; File > Open Menu runs the boot
menu with no card in. Everything else is on the menu bar:

    Ctrl+O   open a card image          F5    pause the simulation
    Ctrl+M   run with no card           F11   fullscreen
    Ctrl+R   restart the simulation     Ctrl+Q   quit
    Ctrl+P   preferences

A key bound to a pad button is pad input; nothing else on the keyboard reaches the emulated
console. Key bindings are stored as SDL keycodes in an INI file:

    $NEMUL_PREFS, or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nemul/nemul.ini, or ~/.config/nemul/nemul.ini
    (%APPDATA%\nemul.ini on Windows)

The first run uses defaults - arrows, Z/X/C for A/B/C, A/S/D for X/Y/Z, Return for Start, Tab
for Mode - and nothing is written until the preferences window is saved.

## Threads

The simulation runs on a thread of its own and the window on the main one, because an
interpreter that manages a third of real time on a heavy program cannot also draw sixty frames
a second from the same core. What the two share is a mutex and a handful of things: the pads
going one way, the newest picture, the status line and the crash box coming back, and a one-slot
channel for "reset" and "put this card in". Everything else - the process table, the interpreter,
the emulated console's memory - belongs to the simulation thread, so the front end is the only
part of the emulator that knows about threads at all.

The window draws only when there is something new to show: a fresh picture, an input event, or a
screenshot to take. Redrawing an unchanged picture sixty times a second keeps a core warm and
takes memory bandwidth away from the simulation, and this emulator's picture often changes only
a handful of times a second.

`NEMUL_LOG_TIMING=1` reports where the time went at exit - wall clock, cpu in the emulation, cpu
in the drawing, and how many emulated milliseconds each host second is worth.

## Debugger

`Debug > Debugger...`, or F7, opens a window on the machine: the process list with each
program counter and the function it is in, the registers and status word of the process on
show, a disassembly around the program counter with the current instruction marked, and the
instructions that process has run most recently. F5 stops and resumes the simulation, F10 runs
one instruction, and while stopped `Step 10` and `Step 100` hurry along a loop.

Addresses are entered as hexadecimal. `Break at PC` sets a breakpoint where the program is
standing; breakpoints stop the whole machine with the instruction under them not yet run, and
resuming lets that instruction go before the breakpoint can catch it again. A breakpoint in a
loop is therefore hit on every pass.

Source positions come from the program's own ELF file, loaded with `Debug > Load Symbols` or
`--symbols <elf>`. That gives the function names and the `file:line` notes beside each
instruction, and the source line at the program counter. What the SDK's toolchain writes is
DWARF debug line information - which file and line each address came from - and symbol tables.
It does not write variable or type information, so the window can say where the program is but
not what the variables hold; the registers and the memory they point at are all there is.

The trace list shows the branch of each instruction that took one, whether the branch was taken
and where to, which is the quickest way to see why a program went where it did.

Nothing in the window touches the machine: the simulation thread fills in a snapshot, the
window draws from a copy of it, and requests go back through atomics. That is why it can be open
while the machine runs at full speed.

## GDB

The emulator serves GDB's remote serial protocol, so a stock `gdb` can drive the machine. It
listens on `Rsp/Port` from the preferences, which is 14292 unless it is changed:

    gdb bin/boot.elf -ex "target remote :14292"

Attaching stops the machine, as attaching to a running program always does, and detaching lets
it run again - by itself, without the emulator having to be told. Every emulated process is a
thread to GDB, numbered and named after the process: `Thread <pid>`, named `pid <pid>`, all of
them in one inferior. That is the whole of the machine's threading - there are no threads inside
a process and no scheduler to inspect, so a thread's registers are its process's registers - and
one inferior is enough, because the machine has one memory and one program running in it. It is
also what makes a restarted machine work: a process that is an inferior of its own is a different
inferior every time the machine starts again, and the breakpoints set in the old one stay there
while the new one runs past them.

What that gives you, per process:

- `info threads` lists them, and `thread <n>` picks one. A process that has just been created
  turns up at the next stop.
- Registers and memory of the selected process: `info registers`, `x`, `print`, `p $pc = ...`,
  `set var`, `display`. The register set is GDB's full ARM one, so `cpsr` and the floating point
  registers are there too - the machine has no floating point unit, so those read as zero and
  writes to them are accepted and forgotten.
- Breakpoints, set with `break`, `tbreak` or `hbreak`, and hardware breakpoints too, since the
  emulator stops on an address either way. A breakpoint is for whichever process reaches the
  the address, unless the client names a thread in the packet, which GDB does only when it means one
  thread rather than the program as a whole. GDB's ARM support moves a breakpoint that lands on a
  function's first instruction to just past the prologue and then steps over that location instead
  of reporting it, so a breakpoint on, say, `_start` stops nowhere: break inside the function, or on
  an address no symbol names, and it stops where it should. This is GDB's doing, not the stub's,
  and happens with any ARM remote target.
- `continue`, `stepi` and `nexti`, each stopping on one instruction's worth of progress and
  reporting which thread stopped and why. Everything else GDB builds on those two - running to
  a temporary breakpoint, stepping out of a function - comes along with them.
- Registers and memory can be written as well as read, so `set var`, `set $r0` and
  `set *(int*)0x1000 = ...` all change the machine.
- Ctrl-C interrupts a running machine and reports SIGINT on the thread that was running.
- `signal`, and the signals a program raises itself: a breakpoint instruction, a data or
  prefetch abort and an alignment check all arrive as the signal they would on real hardware.

Two things GDB is not told, because the machine has nothing to tell it. There are no fork or
exec events: a process that starts another one appears in `info threads` at the next stop rather
than leaving GDB to decide what to follow. And there are no watchpoints, since the emulator has
no way to trap a write to an address.

`monitor threads` lists the processes as the emulator sees them, and `monitor prep` resets the
process table as if a game had just been booted. A process has no parent, no working directory
and no command line to report, so those columns stay empty rather than being filled in with
guesses.

## Running a new build

Rebuilding a program and starting the new one should not mean restarting the emulator, so the
same connection can be told to start the machine again with a different image - or with the same
one, read from disk as it is now:

    (gdb) target extended-remote :14292
    (gdb) set remote exec-file /path/to/img/game.iso
    (gdb) run                      restarts the machine with that image, as it is on disk
    (gdb) break main
    (gdb) continue                 boots, and stops in the program you just built

`run` is GDB's own command and goes through the protocol's `vRun`, so it also works for an `.nne`
given as the exec file, which runs on its own with the boot menu out of the way. Between runs,
`monitor reload` does the same thing as `run` without starting the machine going again, which is
the useful form when the breakpoints are already where they belong:

    (gdb) monitor reload           restarts the machine with the loaded image, as it is now
    (gdb) continue                 stops in the new build at the same breakpoints

Breakpoints belong to the machine rather than to a process, so they survive that restart: the
emulator keeps them, and puts them back into the process table it has just thrown away. The
machine stands still after a restart asked for by a client, so that the breakpoints go in before
the new program runs past them. `monitor run <file>` boots a particular image, `monitor image`
says which one is loaded, and `qXfer:exec-file:read` tells GDB the same, so `info inferiors`
shows the file the machine was started from.



GDB's own end of it can be checked without a window too: the emulator is happy with either a
real `gdb` or a script that speaks the protocol, and both work with `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy`.
`NEMUL_LOG_RSP=1` puts every protocol decision worth knowing about on stderr - what was loaded,
which breakpoint went in and for whom, what was resumed and what was reported - because a client
shows you none of it and a session that stops making sense is otherwise guesswork.

The front end can be driven where there is no display, which is how it is checked on a build
machine. `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy` selects SDL's headless video driver, and the rest of the
options script a run:

    --image <iso>               insert a card image at startup
    --frames <n>                draw n frames and exit
    --shot <file.bmp>           write the last frame to a BMP
    --ui prefs|files|crash      open that window at startup
    --debug                     open the debugger at startup
    --symbols <elf>             load function names and source lines, and open the debugger
    --break <hex address>       stop at an address, and open the debugger
    --step <n>                  start stopped and run n instructions first
    --ui-key <key>:<frame>[:<n>]
                                push a key into the window itself once n frames have been
                                drawn, for driving menus and the debugger; repeatable
    --press <key>:<ms>[:<ms>]
                                push a real SDL key event once the console has reached that
                                many emulated milliseconds, and let go after <ms>; repeatable

Presses are timed in *emulated* milliseconds, not in frames: the window draws at its own speed
and the console at its own, so only the console's clock makes a scripted run repeatable.

For example, to boot the `showinput` example, dismiss the saved-data prompt with START, hold the
A button and photograph the result:

    SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy ./bin/nemul.elf showinput.iso --frames 900 \
        --press Return:3000:200 --press z:5000:20000 --shot /tmp/showinput.bmp

`NEMUL_LOG_INPUT` prints pad state changes to stderr, like the core's other `NEMUL_LOG_*`
switches.


Note that the interpreter runs at roughly one emulated millisecond per host millisecond on a
current machine for a game, and a good deal slower for the boot menu, which spends its time
probing for a card. Whatever it manages, the window keeps drawing: the simulation runs behind
it at its own speed and drops emulated time it cannot keep up with, rather than the window
waiting for it.

## Layout

    src/nemul.cpp          main loop, window, menu bar, status line, crash box, screenshot
    src/ui_prefs.cpp/.h    preferences window and key binding (was cfgwin.cpp + bindprompt.cpp)
    src/ui_files.cpp/.h    in-window disk image picker (Dear ImGui has no file dialog)
    src/prefs.cpp          INI preferences (was wxConfig)
    src/nemul_headless.cpp console front end (PPM frame dumps, --bp/--step)
    src/prefs_headless.cpp no-op preferences for the console build
    third_party/imgui      vendored Dear ImGui 1.93.0, see README.vendored.md there

The wxWidgets front end this replaces is in the git history, along with the wxWidgets tarball
and `wx_build.sh` that used to be needed to build it.
